// K112 — design 275 U4, the cause `blocking` where it meets `cooperative` in
// ONE std method, in the one context that tells the two apart.
//
// `Command.output` parks cooperatively on the child's exit AND offloads the pipe
// drain through a `blocking` extern. Design 242 ruling 9 permits the blocking
// half in a `Thread.spawn` body and refuses every other cause, so the answer
// here is REFUSAL — a cooperative park on a thread with no executor to resume it
// is the hazard that ruling exists for.
//
// A std body belongs to another typechecker's graph, so the entry compile knows
// this method through a SEEDED LEAF. While that leaf carried ONE representative
// cause, the thread-body question was answered by whichever of the two the
// label happened to name: this program COMPILED AND RAN on a compiler without
// design 275 U4, parking cooperatively on a thread that cannot resume it. The
// leaf carries the whole cause SET now.
//
// THE MESSAGE IS THE SECOND HALF OF THE ROW. The refusal STRIKES the blocking
// cause (it is the permitted one), so a leaf that spells only its blocking
// source leaves the diagnostic with nothing to name — it printed ``closure calls
// <suspension source>``, a placeholder, in a user-facing message. The leaf
// carries one source per cause now, representative FIRST, so a reader that
// strikes nothing still sees exactly what it always saw (an ordinary `sync`
// violation through this method names the blocking extern) while this one names
// the method and the cooperative primitive it parks on.
//
// It is its OWN file, not a third cell in K114, because a compile stops after a
// settling that reported errors and this refusal is only reached at a later one
// (SL-327): beside another refused cell its message is never emitted.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: cannot suspend in a `Thread.spawn { ... }` body
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: closure calls `Command.output` → io_wait
// EXPECT-ERROR-ABSENT: <suspension source>
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: a spawned thread runs no executor

import std.process.{Command}

func main() {
    var c = Command(program: "/bin/echo")
    try! c.arg("hi")
    var d = Thread.spawn { [move c] in
        let _ = c.output()
        3
    }
    print(d.join())
}
